    "q-225007": {
      "dom_id": "step-q225007",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "**Fully** factor the following trinomial.\n\n$6{u}^{2}+34u+20=$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$u\\,+2)(u\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n6{u}^{2}+34u+20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms in the trinomial have a common factor of $2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $2:$\n\n$$\n2(3{u}^{2}+17u+10)\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $3{u}^{2}+17u+10$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3\\times 10=30$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($17$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $30.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $30$ are\n\n$$\n(1,30),\\,(2,15),\\,(3,10),\\,(6,5).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(2,15)$ add to $17:$ \n\n$$\n2+15=17\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $2u+15u,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+17u+10 & = \\\\ 3{u}^{2}+2u+15u+10 & = \\\\ 3{u}^{2}+15u+2u+10 & = \\\\ (3{u}^{2}+15u)+(2u+10) & = \\\\ 3u(u+5)+2(u+5) & = \\\\ (3u+2)(u+5) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{u}^{2}+34u+20= \\\\ 2(3{u}^{2}+17u+10)= \\\\ 2(3u+2)(u+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
