    "q-225283": {
      "dom_id": "step-q225283",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$4{w}^{2}-30w-16=$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n4{w}^{2}-30w-16\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2{w}^{2}-15w-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2{w}^{2}-15w-8$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2\\times -8=-16$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-15$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-16$ are\n\n$$\n(1,-16),\\,(-1,16),\\,(2,-8),\\,(-2,8),\\,(4,-4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,-16)$ add to $-15:$ \n\n$$\n1-16=-15\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $w-16w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{w}^{2}-15w-8 & = \\\\ 2{w}^{2}+w-16w-8 & = \\\\ (2{w}^{2}+w)-(16w+8) & = \\\\ w(2w+1)-8(2w+1) & = \\\\ (w-8)(2w+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(w-8)(2w+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
